<exercise label="ex-continuous-pdf-all-mc">
    <title>Properties of Continuous Density Functions</title>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            Consider a continuous random variable <m>Y</m> defined by the probability density function (PDF):
            <me>f_Y(y) = ky^2, \quad \text{for } 0 \le y \le 2</me>,
            where <m>k</m> is a constant. 
        </p>
    </introduction>

    <!-- Part A: Solving for k -->
    <task xml:id="task-mc-solve-k">
        <statement>
            <p>
                What must the value of the constant <m>k</m> equal for <m>f_Y(y)</m> to be a mathematically valid probability density function?
            </p>
        </statement>
        <choices>
            <choice><statement><p><m>k = 1/2</m></p></statement>
                <feedback><p>Incorrect. Remember that the total area under the curve from <m>y=0</m> to <m>y=2</m> must integrate to exactly 1.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p><m>k = 1/8</m></p></statement>
                <feedback><p>Incorrect. It looks like you evaluated the anti-derivative boundaries but forgot to incorporate the denominator from the power rule integration.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice correct="yes"><statement><p><m>k = 3/8</m></p></statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>Correct! Using the total area property:</p>
                    <p><me>\int_{0}^{2} ky^2 \, dy = \left[ \frac{k y^3}{3} \right]_{0}^{2} = \frac{8k}{3} - 0 = 1 \implies k = \frac{3}{8}</me></p>
                </feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p><m>k = 3/4</m></p></statement>
                <feedback><p>Incorrect. Double-check your calculation when evaluating <m>y^3</m> at the upper bound of 2.</p></feedback></choice>
        </choices>
    </task>

    <!-- Part B: Expected Value -->
    <task xml:id="task-mc-expected-value">
        <statement>
            <p>
                 Compute the expected value <m>E(Y)</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <choices>
            <choice><statement><p><m>E(Y) = 1.00</m></p></statement>
                <feedback><p>Incorrect. Remember that for a continuous variable, <m>E(Y) = \int y \cdot f_Y(y) \, dy</m>, which shifts the integrand power to <m>y^3</m>.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice correct="yes"><statement><p><m>E(Y) = 1.50</m></p></statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>Correct! Setting up the expectation integral:</p>
                    <p><me>E(Y) = \int_{0}^{2} y \cdot \left(\frac{3}{8}y^2\right) \, dy = \frac{3}{8} \int_{0}^{2} y^3 \, dy = \frac{3}{8} \left[ \frac{y^4}{4} \right]_{0}^{2} = \frac{3}{8} \left(\frac{16}{4}\right) = 1.5</me></p>
                </feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p><m>E(Y) = 1.33</m></p></statement>
                <feedback><p>Incorrect. This looks like the result of calculating the anti-derivative without scaling by the constant factor <m>k = 3/8</m>.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p><m>E(Y) = 1.75</m></p></statement>
                <feedback><p>Incorrect. Double-check your evaluation steps when raising the upper bound to the 4th power.</p></feedback></choice>
        </choices>
    </task>

    <!-- Part C: Variance -->
    <task xml:id="task-mc-variance">
        <statement>
            <p>
                 Compute the variance <m>V(Y)</m> for this random variable.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <choices>
            <choice correct="yes"><statement><p><m>V(Y) = 0.15</m></p></statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>Correct! First, compute the intermediate term <m>E(Y^2)</m>:</p>
                    <p><me>E(Y^2) = \int_{0}^{2} y^2 \cdot \left(\frac{3}{8}y^2\right) \, dy = \frac{3}{8} \left[ \frac{y^5}{5} \right]_{0}^{2} = \frac{3}{8} \left(\frac{32}{5}\right) = \frac{12}{5} = 2.4</me></p>
                    <p>Now, use the variance identity <m>V(Y) = E(Y^2) - [E(Y)]^2</m>:</p>
                    <p><me>V(Y) = 2.4 - (1.5)^2 = 2.4 - 2.25 = 0.15</me></p>
                </feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p><m>V(Y) = 2.40</m></p></statement>
                <feedback><p>Incorrect. This is the value of <m>E(Y^2)</m> before subtracting the squared mean.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p><m>V(Y) = 0.25</m></p></statement>
                <feedback><p>Incorrect. Make sure to square the mean (<m>1.5^2 = 2.25</m>) when applying the variance formula subtraction step.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p><m>V(Y) = 0.90</m></p></statement>
                <feedback><p>Incorrect. Ensure you use the formula <m>E(Y^2) - [E(Y)]^2</m> instead of subtracting the raw mean value from <m>E(Y^2)</m>.</p></feedback></choice>
        </choices>
    </task>

    <!-- Part D: Probability Interval -->
    <task xml:id="task-mc-interval">
        <statement>
            <p>
                 Find the probability <m>P(1 \le Y \le 2)</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <choices>
            <choice><statement><p><m>0.500</m></p></statement>
                <feedback><p>Incorrect. Remember that the distribution is skewed toward higher values because of the quadratic weight, so the upper half of the range contains a larger share of the probability.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p><m>0.125</m></p></statement>
                <feedback><p>Incorrect. This is the lower tail probability <m>P(0 \le Y \le 1)</m>. Subtract this value from 1 to find the correct upper tail area.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice correct="yes"><statement><p><m>0.875</m></p></statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>Correct! Integrating between the specified boundaries:</p>
                    <p><me>P(1 \le Y \le 2) = \int_{1}^{2} \frac{3}{8}y^2 \, dy = \left[ \frac{y^3}{8} \right]_{1}^{2} = \frac{2^3}{8} - \frac{1^3}{8} = 1 - \frac{1}{8} = 0.875</me></p>
                </feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p><m>0.750</m></p></statement>
                <feedback><p>Incorrect. Double-check your subtraction arithmetic when plugging the lower bound into your anti-derivative tool.</p></feedback></choice>
        </choices>
    </task>

    <!-- Part E: CDF Derivation (Now Multiple Choice) -->
    <task xml:id="task-mc-cdf-derivation">
        <statement>
            <p>
                 Find the cumulative distribution function (CDF), <m>F_Y(y)</m>, for the interval <m>0 \le y \le 2</m>. 
            </p>
        </statement>
        <choices>
            <choice><statement><p><m>F_Y(y) = \frac{y^3}{3}</m></p></statement>
                <feedback><p>Incorrect. You integrated <m>y^2</m> correctly to get <m>\frac{y^3}{3}</m>, but you forgot to multiply by the constant multiplier <m>k = \frac{3}{8}</m>.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice correct="yes"><statement><p><m>F_Y(y) = \frac{y^3}{8}</m></p></statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>Correct! To find the general cumulative distribution function, integrate the PDF from the lower limit 0 up to an arbitrary point <m>y</m>:</p>
                    <p><me>F_Y(y) = \int_{0}^{y} \frac{3}{8}t^2 \, dt = \left[ \frac{t^3}{8} \right]_{0}^{y} = \frac{y^3}{8}</me></p>
                    <p>Evaluating this function at the boundaries confirms the probability from Part D: <m>F_Y(2) - F_Y(1) = \frac{8}{8} - \frac{1}{8} = 0.875</m>.</p>
                </feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p><m>F_Y(y) = \frac{3y^3}{8}</m></p></statement>
                <feedback><p>Incorrect. It looks like you forgot to apply the power rule integration denominator (<m>3</m>) to cancel out the numerator coefficient when taking the anti-derivative.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p><m>F_Y(y) = \frac{y^2}{4}</m></p></statement>
                <feedback><p>Incorrect. Remember that integrating a function increases the polynomial power by 1 (<m>y^2 \rightarrow y^3</m>).</p></feedback></choice>
        </choices>
    </task>
</exercise>






<exercise label="ex-continuous-ev-charging-capacity">
    <title>Green Infrastructure: EV Charging Network Utilization</title>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            The proportion of maximum daily grid capacity utilized during peak hours across a city's public electric vehicle (EV) charging network is modeled by a continuous random variable <m>X</m> with the probability density function (PDF) given by:
            <me>f_X(x) = 12x^2(1-x), \quad \text{for } 0 \le x \le 1</me>
            and <m>f_X(x) = 0</m> elsewhere. Grid utilization must stay above  40% (<m>x \gt 0.40</m>) on any given day for the network to operate without a financial loss. Otherwise, maintenance overhead outpaces charging revenue. Complete the tasks below to evaluate the network's performance.
        </p>
    </introduction>

    <!-- Part A: Probability of Being Profitable / Operating at a Loss (Multiple Choice) -->
    <task xml:id="task-ev-prob-loss">
        <statement>
            <p>
                 Use the probability density function (PDF) to determine the probability that the network operates at a financial loss on a randomly selected day.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <choices>
            <choice><statement><p><m>0.8208</m></p></statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>Incorrect. This is the probability that the network is profitable, <m>P(X \gt 0.40)</m>. The question asks for the probability of operating at a loss, which requires checking the area below or equal to 40% utilization.</p>
                </feedback></choice>
            <choice correct="yes"><statement><p><m>0.1792</m></p></statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>Correct! Set up the definite integral from the lower boundary of 0 to the financial loss threshold of 0.40:</p>
                    <p><me>P(X \le 0.40) = \int_{0}^{0.40} 12x^2(1-x) \, dx = \int_{0}^{0.40} (12x^2 - 12x^3) \, dx</me></p>
                    <p><me>= \left[ 4x^3 - 3x^4 \right]_{0}^{0.40} = 4(0.40)^3 - 3(0.40)^4 = 0.256 - 0.0768 = 0.1792</me></p>
                </feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p><m>0.2560</m></p></statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>Incorrect. It looks like you evaluated the <m>4x^3</m> term but forgot to subtract the <m>3x^4</m> term during the definite integration steps.</p>
                </feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p><m>0.0768</m></p></statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>Incorrect. This matches only the subtracted part of the anti-derivative expansion (<m>3x^4</m>). Review your definite integral evaluation steps.</p>
                </feedback></choice>
        </choices>
    </task>

    <!-- Part B: Interactive CDF Expression Input -->
    <task xml:id="task-ev-cdf-input">
        <statement>
            <p>
                  Find the cumulative distribution function (CDF), <m>F_X(x)</m>, over the domain interval <m>0 \le x \le 1</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
            <m>F_X(x) = </m> <fillin mode="math" width="15" answer="4x^3 - 3x^4"/>
        </p>
        </statement>
        <evaluation>
        <evaluate>
            <test correct="yes">
                <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
            </test>
            <feedback>
            <p>
                Correct! To find the general cumulative distribution function, integrate the PDF from the lower limit 0 up to an arbitrary variable boundary <m>x</m>:
                <me>F_X(x) = \int_{0}^{x} (12t^2 - 12t^3) \, dt = \left[ 4t^3 - 3t^4 \right]_{0}^{x} = 4x^3 - 3x^4, \quad \text{for } 0 \le x \le 1</me>
            </p>
            <p>
                Evaluating your derived CDF directly confirms Part A: <m>F_X(0.40) = 4(0.40)^3 - 3(0.40)^4 = 0.1792</m>.
            </p>
        </feedback>
        </evaluate>
    </evaluation> 
        
    </task>

    <!-- Part C: Expected Value (Multiple Choice) -->
    <task xml:id="task-ev-expected-value">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Find the expected value for the peak capacity utilization across this charging network.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <choices>
            <choice><statement><p><m>0.50</m></p></statement>
                <feedback><p>Incorrect. While the parameter bounds span 0 to 1, the distribution is asymmetric, meaning the true center of mass is shifted higher than the basic interval midpoint.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice correct="yes"><statement><p><m> 0.60</m></p></statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>Correct! Setting up the continuous expectation integral: </p>
                    <p><me>E(X) = \int_{0}^{1} x \cdot 12x^2(1-x) \, dx = \int_{0}^{1} (12x^3 - 12x^4) \, dx</me></p>
                    <p><me>= \left[ 3x^4 - \frac{12}{5}x^5 \right]_{0}^{1} = 3(1)^4 - 2.4(1)^5 = 3 - 2.4 = 0.60</me></p>
                    <p>This means the network averages 60% capacity utilization during peak hours.</p>
                </feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p><m>0.40</m></p></statement>
                <feedback><p>Incorrect. You may have confused the expected value with the maximum allowable threshold for operating at a loss introduced in Part A.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p><m> 0.75</m></p></statement>
                <feedback><p>Incorrect. Review your integration anti-derivative powers. Increasing the integrand by a factor of <m>x</m> turns the components into <m>12x^3</m> and <m>-12x^4</m>.</p></feedback></choice>
        </choices>
    </task>

    <!-- Part D: Variance (Multiple Choice) -->
    <task xml:id="task-ev-variance">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Find the variance <m>V(X)</m> for the network's peak capacity utilization.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <choices>
            <choice correct="yes"><statement><p><m> 0.04</m></p></statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>Correct! First, compute the second moment value <m>E(X^2)</m>:</p>
                    <p><me>E(X^2) = \int_{0}^{1} x^2 \cdot 12x^2(1-x) \, dx = \int_{0}^{1} (12x^4 - 12x^5) \, dx</me></p>
                    <p><me>= \left[ \frac{12}{5}x^5 - 2x^6 \right]_{0}^{1} = 2.4(1)^5 - 2(1)^6 = 2.4 - 2 = 0.40</me></p>
                    <p>Now apply the variance identity formula <m>V(X) = E(X^2) - [E(X)]^2</m>:</p>
                    <p><me>V(X) = 0.40 - (0.60)^2 = 0.40 - 0.36 = 0.04</me></p>
                </feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p><m>0.40</m></p></statement>
                <feedback><p>Incorrect. This is the value of the second moment <m>E(X^2)</m> alone. You still need to subtract the square of your mean (<m>0.60^2 = 0.36</m>).</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p><m> 0.20</m></p></statement>
                <feedback><p>Incorrect. Ensure you subtract the squared mean (<m>0.36</m>) from <m>E(X^2)</m>, rather than subtracting the raw unsquared mean value.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p><m> 0.16</m></p></statement>
                <feedback><p>Incorrect. Double-check your subtraction arithmetic when calculating <m>0.40 - 0.36</m>.</p></feedback></choice>
        </choices>
    </task>
</exercise>


  <!-- ==================================================================== -->
  <!-- EXERCISE 2: CONTINUOUS PROBABILITY SYSTEM                            -->
  <!-- ==================================================================== -->
  <exercise xml:id="ex-int-continuous-prob">
    <title>Interactive Analysis: Continuous Asset Metrics</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        While the data in <xref ref="ex-int-discrete-prob"/> aggregates timelines into integers, true regulatory pipeline terminations happen continuously down to the exact day. Analysts use a continuous random variable <m>X</m> (measured in years) to evaluate precision biological hardware lifelines spanning an operational domain from 8 to 20 years.
      </p>
      <p>
        The probability density function (PDF) for <m>X</m> is defined as:
        <me>
          f(x) = \begin{cases} \frac{1}{72}(20 - x) \amp \text{for } 8 \le x \le 20 \\ 0 \amp \text{elsewhere} \end{cases}
        </me>
      </p>
    </introduction>
    
    <introduction>
      <p>Analyze the continuous density behavior to answer the following problems.</p>
    </introduction>

    <!-- Part A: Interactive CDF Identification -->
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Determine the correct mathematical structure of the Cumulative Distribution Function (CDF), <m>F(x) = P(X \le x)</m>, across the active operational interval <m>8 \le x \le 20</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <choices>
        <choice>
          <statement><p><m>F(x) = \frac{1}{72}(20x - \frac{x^2}{2})</m></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>Incorrect. This expression forgets to subtract the definite lower-bound accumulation constant evaluated at the start point <m>x = 8</m>.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement><p><m>F(x) = 1 - \frac{(20 - x)^2}{144}</m></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>Correct! Integrating <m>\int_{8}^{x} \frac{1}{72}(20-t)\,dt</m> and evaluating terms yields <m>1 - \frac{(20-x)^2}{144}</m>. Note that evaluating at <m>x=8</m> returns 0, and <m>x=20</m> returns 1, matching boundary standards perfectly.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p><m>F(x) = \frac{(20 - x)^2}{144}</m></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>Incorrect. This expression evaluates to the survival probability function <m>P(X \gt x)</m> rather than the left-tailed cumulative distribution probability.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </task>

    <!-- Part B: Interactive Decimal Calculation Verification -->
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Using the structural CDF established above, compute the probability that a random continuous bio-hardware patent line operates for <strong>more than 12 years</strong>: <m>P(X \gt 12)</m>. Express your response as a fraction or a decimal rounded to four places.
        </p>
            <p>
        <fillin width="8" answer="0.4444"/>
      </p>
    </statement>
  
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Splendid! Using the complement rule with our CDF framework: <m>P(X \gt 12) = 1 - F(12) = 1 - [1 - \frac{(20-12)^2}{144}] = \frac{8^2}{144} = \frac{64}{144} = \frac{4}{9} \approx 0.4444</m>.</p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
        </evaluate>
      </evaluation>
    </task>
  </exercise>


<exercise label="ex-continuous-drone-battery">
    <title>Reliability Engineering: Drone Battery Lifespans</title>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            The continuous operational lifespan before failure (measured in hundreds of hours) for a high-performance drone battery pack is modeled by a continuous random variable <m>Y</m> with the cumulative distribution function (CDF):
            <me>F_Y(y) = 1 - e^{-y^2}, \quad y \le 0</me>
            Complete the multiple-choice tasks below to analyze the reliability profile of these battery components.
        </p>
    </introduction>

    <!-- Task A: At least 200 hours -->
    <task xml:id="task-drone-at-least-200">
        <statement>
            <p>
                 Find the probability that a randomly selected drone battery pack operates continuously for at least 200 hours.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <choices>
            <choice><statement><p><m>0.9817</m></p></statement>
                <feedback><p>Incorrect. This is the probability that the battery fails <em>before</em> 200 hours, <m>P(Y \le 2) = F_Y(2)</m>. You need the complement for "at least".</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice correct="yes"><statement><p><m>0.0183</m></p></statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>Correct! Since the time unit is in hundreds of hours, 200 hours corresponds to <m>y = 2</m>.</p>
                    <p>Using the complement rule: 
                    <me>P(Y \ge 2) = 1 - F_Y(2) = 1 - (1 - e^{-2^2}) = e^{-4} \approx 0.0183</me></p>
                </feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p><m>0.1353</m></p></statement>
                <feedback><p>Incorrect. It looks like you forgot to square the time parameter in the exponent, computing <m>e^{-2}</m> instead of <m>e^{-4}</m>.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p><m>0.3679</m></p></statement>
                <feedback><p>Incorrect. This is the probability of lasting at least 100 hours (<m>y=1</m>). Recompute using the target boundary for 200 hours.</p></feedback></choice>
        </choices>
    </task>

    <!-- Task B: Conditional Probability -->
    <task xml:id="task-drone-conditional">
        <statement>
            <p>
                 What is the probability that a drone battery pack operates for more than 100 hours given that it operates for at most 200 hours? In other words, evaluate the conditional probability: <m>P(Y \gt 1 \mid Y \le 2)</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <choices>
            <choice><statement><p><m>0.3496</m></p></statement>
                <feedback><p>Incorrect. This is only the numerator value, which is the unconditional interval probability <m>P(1 \lt Y \le 2)</m>. You must divide this by the condition space <m>P(Y \le 2)</m>.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice correct="yes"><statement><p><m>0.3561</m> </p></statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>Correct! Using the conditional probability identity:</p>
                    <p><me>P(Y \gt 1 \mid Y \le 2) = \frac{P(1 \lt Y \le 2)}{P(Y \le 2)} = \frac{F_Y(2) - F_Y(1)}{F_Y(2)}</me></p>
                    <p>Evaluating the individual components:
                    <md>
                        <mrow>F_Y(1) \amp = 1 - e^{-1} \approx 0.6321</mrow>
                        <mrow>F_Y(2) \amp = 1 - e^{-4} \approx 0.9817</mrow>
                    </md>
                    Substituting back into the formula:
                    <me>\frac{0.9817 - 0.6321}{0.9817} = \frac{0.3496}{0.9817} \approx 0.3561 \approx 0.36</me></p>
                </feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p><m>0.6321</m></p></statement>
                <feedback><p>Incorrect. This is the baseline probability of failure occurring within the first 100 hours, <m>F_Y(1)</m>.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p><m>0.5000</m></p></statement>
                <feedback><p>Incorrect. Review the conditional division rules for non-linear exponential distribution subsets.</p></feedback></choice>
        </choices>
    </task>

    <!-- Task C: PDF Identification -->
    <task xml:id="task-drone-pdf-id">
        <statement>
            <p>
                 Which of the following expressions represents the mathematically valid probability density function (PDF), <m>f_Y(y)</m>, derived from the baseline battery model?
            </p>
        </statement>
        <choices>
            <choice><statement><p><m>f_Y(y) = e^{-y^2}, \quad y \le 0</m></p></statement>
                <feedback><p>Incorrect. This leaves out the necessary chain rule derivative multiplier when dealing with the variable inside the exponent.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice correct="yes"><statement><p><m>f_Y(y) = 2ye^{-y^2}, \quad y \le 0</m></p></statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>Correct! To obtain the probability density function <m>f_Y(y)</m> from the cumulative distribution function <m>F_Y(y)</m>, apply the first derivative with respect to <m>y</m> using the chain rule:</p>
                    <p><me>f_Y(y) = \frac{d}{dy}[1 - e^{-y^2}] = 0 - e^{-y^2} \cdot \left(\frac{d}{dy}[-y^2]\right) = -e^{-y^2} \cdot (-2y) = 2ye^{-y^2}</me></p>
                </feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p><m>f_Y(y) = -2ye^{-y^2}, \quad y \le 0</m></p></statement>
                <feedback><p>Incorrect. A valid probability density function cannot evaluate to negative values over its valid domain support. Watch your signs when subtracting the internal derivative.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p><m>f_Y(y) = y^2 e^{-y^2}, \quad y \le 0</m></p></statement>
                <feedback><p>Incorrect. Remember that the power rule derivative of <m>y^2</m> yields <m>2y</m>, not <m>y^2</m>.</p></feedback></choice>
        </choices>
    </task>
</exercise>




<exercise label="ex-continuous-adhesive-matching-mc" xmlns:pi="http://pretextbook.org">
    <title>Materials Engineering: Adhesive Curing Time Analysis</title>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            The curing time (in hours) of a specialized aerospace structural adhesive sealant is modeled by a continuous random variable <m>Y</m> with the probability density function (PDF):
            <me>f_Y(y) = \frac{3}{8}(7-y)^2, \quad \text{for } 5 \le y \le 7</me>
            and <m>f_Y(y) = 0</m> elsewhere. Complete the interactive tasks below to evaluate this material.
        </p>
    </introduction>

    <!-- Combined Parts A & B: Matching Problem -->
    <task xml:id="task-curing-matching">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Match the requested statistical metric for the adhesive curing time  with its correct numerical calculation. 
            </p>
 </statement>
                <cardsort>
                    <match>
                        <premise><p> <m>\mu_Y</m></p></premise>
                        <response><p><m>5.5</m></p></response>
                    </match>
                    <match>
                        <premise><p><m>\sigma_Y</m></p></premise>
                        <response><p><m>0.34</m></p></response>
                    </match>
                    <match>
                        
                        <response><p><m>0.15</m></p></response>
                    </match>
                    <match>
                        <response><p><m>6.0 </m></p></response>
                    </match>
                    <match>
                        <response><p><m>5.75 </m></p></response>
                    </match>
                </cardsort>
         
    
        <solution>
            <p>
                <strong>Expected Value Derivation:</strong> Evaluate <m>E(Y) = \int_{5}^{7} y \cdot \frac{3}{8}(7-y)^2 \, dy</m>. Substituting <m>u = 7-y</m> gives:
                <me>\frac{3}{8} \int_{0}^{2} (7u^2 - u^3) \, du = \frac{3}{8} \left[ \frac{7}{3}u^3 - \frac{1}{4}u^4 \right]_{0}^{2} = \frac{3}{8} \left( \frac{56}{3} - 4 \right) = 5.5 \text{ hours}</me>
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>Standard Deviation Derivation:</strong> First evaluate the second moment <m>E(Y^2) = \int_{5}^{7} y^2 \cdot \frac{3}{8}(7-y)^2 \, dy = 30.4</m>. 
                Then find variance: <m>V(Y) = 30.4 - (5.5)^2 = 0.15</m>. Taking the square root yields <m>\sigma = \sqrt{0.15} \approx 0.39 \text{ hours}</m>.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </task>

    <!-- Part C: Contextual Interpretation (Multiple Choice) -->
    <task xml:id="task-curing-interpretation-mc">
        <statement>
            <p>
                 Which of the following statements provides the most accurate interpretation of the mean and standard deviation for this adhesive?
            </p>
           
        </statement>
        <choices>
                <choice correct="yes">
                    <statement>
                        <p>The adhesive requires a long-run average curing time of <m>\mu_Y</m> hours per batch, with a typical operational variation of roughly <m>\sigma_Y</m> hours around that mean.</p>
                    </statement>
                    <feedback>
                        <p>Correct! The mean represents the long-run center of mass for curing times, while the standard deviation provides a measure of typical spread or variability in the same units (hours).</p>
                    </feedback>
                </choice>
                <choice>
                    <statement>
                        <p>Every single batch of adhesive will take exactly <m>\mu_Y</m> hours to cure, but up to <m>\sigma_Y</m>% of batches will experience material failure.</p>
                    </statement>
                    <feedback>
                        <p>Incorrect. In a continuous random variable model, individual batches will vary due to a smooth probability curve. The standard deviation describes physical spread, not a deterministic percentage of defective units.</p>
                    </feedback>
                </choice>
                <choice>
                    <statement>
                        <p>The absolute minimum curing time required is <m>\mu_Y</m> hours, and the standard deviation indicates that all batches will finish curing by <m>\mu_Y</m><m>+\sigma_Y</m> hours.</p>
                    </statement>
                    <feedback>
                        <p>Incorrect. The support of the density function establishes that the minimum baseline curing time is 5.0 hours, and batches can legally take up to 7.0 hours to cure.</p>
                    </feedback>
                </choice>
                <choice>
                    <statement>
                        <p>The network averages a <m>\mu_Y</m>% baseline optimization efficiency rate, while experiencing a strict <m>\sigma_Y</m> minute scheduling penalty per manufacturing shift.</p>
                    </statement>
                    <feedback>
                        <p>Incorrect. This statement changes the units entirely and applies an out-of-context operational framework. Keep your interpretation grounded strictly in the hours required for chemical curing.</p>
                    </feedback>
                </choice>
</choices>>
    </task>
</exercise>

  </exercises>




  <exercises xml:id="hw-cont-rv-turn-in" >
   <title>Written Exercises</title>
   <introduction>
     <p>
       Write intro to written part of homework set.....
     </p>
   </introduction>




    <exercise label="ex-continuous-fiber-strength">
        <title>Breaking Strength Analysis</title>
        <introduction>
          <p>

        The breaking strength (in Newtons) of a new grade of advanced structural carbon fiber cables is modeled by a continuous random variable <m>X</m> with the cumulative distribution function:
        <me>F_X(x) = 1 - kx^{-3}, \quad x \gt 44000</me>
        where <m>k</m> is a positive proportionality constant. Complete the analytical derivations below to evaluate this structural model.

          </p>
        </introduction>
        <!-- Part A: Deriving PDF from CDF -->
        <task xml:id="task-fiber-pdf">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Show that the probability density function (PDF) for <m>X</m> is given by:
                    <me>f_X(x) = 3kx^{-4}, \quad x \gt 44000</me>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    To find the probability density function <m>f_X(x)</m> from the cumulative distribution function <m>F_X(x)</m>, we take the first derivative with respect to <m>x</m>:
                    <md>
                        <mrow>f_X(x) \amp = \frac{d}{dx}[F_X(x)]</mrow>
                        <mrow>\amp = \frac{d}{dx}[1 - kx^{-3}]</mrow>
                        <mrow>\amp = 0 - k \cdot (-3)x^{-4}</mrow>
                        <mrow>\amp = 3kx^{-4}, \quad \text{for } x \gt 44000</mrow>
                    </md>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <!-- Part B: Solving for Constant k -->
        <task xml:id="task-fiber-constant">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Show that the value of the proportionality constant <m>k</m> must be  <m>44000^3</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    By definition, the support of the distribution begins at the lower boundary <m>x = 44000</m>. Because no cables can break at a force less than this threshold, the cumulative probability at this boundary must equal zero (<m>F_X(44000) = 0</m>):
                    <md>
                        <mrow>1 - k(44000)^{-3} \amp = 0</mrow>
                        <mrow>1 \amp = \frac{k}{44000^3}</mrow>
                        <mrow>k \amp = 44000^3</mrow>
                    </md>
                    Alternatively, using total area under the density function curve:
                    <md>
                        <mrow>\int_{44000}^{\infty} 3kx^{-4} \, dx \amp = 1</mrow>
                        <mrow>\left[ -kx^{-3} \right]_{44000}^{\infty} \amp = 1</mrow>
                        <mrow>0 - \left( -k(44000)^{-3} \right) \amp = 1 \implies k = 44000^3</mrow>
                    </md>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <!-- Part C: Finding the Mean -->
        <task xml:id="task-fiber-mean">
            <statement>
                <p>
                     Show that the mean breaking strength, given by this model, is <m>\mu = E(X) = 66,000</m> Newtons.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    The expected value is computed by integrating <m>x \cdot f_X(x)</m> over the domain:
                    <md>
                        <mrow>E(X) \amp = \int_{44000}^{\infty} x \cdot \left(3kx^{-4}\right) \, dx</mrow>
                        <mrow>\amp = 3k \int_{44000}^{\infty} x^{-3} \, dx</mrow>
                        <mrow>\amp = 3k \left[ \frac{x^{-2}}{-2} \right]_{44000}^{\infty}</mrow>
                        <mrow>\amp = -\frac{3k}{2} \left[ 0 - (44000)^{-2} \right]</mrow>
                        <mrow>\amp = \frac{3k}{2 \cdot 44000^2}</mrow>
                    </md>
                    Substituting <m>k = 44000^3</m> into our simplified equation:
                    <md>
                        <mrow>E(X) \amp = \frac{3 \cdot 44000^3}{2 \cdot 44000^2}</mrow>
                        <mrow>\amp = \frac{3}{2} \cdot 44000</mrow>
                        <mrow>\amp = 1.5 \cdot 44000 = 66000 \text{ Newtons}</mrow>
                    </md>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <!-- Part D: Evaluating Proportion Under Mean -->
        <task xml:id="task-fiber-proportion">
            <statement>
                <p>
                     Show that the theoretical proportion of structural cables possessing a breaking strength less than the calculated mean is approximately 70%.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    We need to find the cumulative probability evaluated at the mean value, <m>P(X \lt 66000) = F_X(66000)</m>. Substituting <m>k = 44000^3</m> into the original CDF formula:
                    <md>
                        <mrow>F_X(66000) \amp = 1 - 44000^3 \cdot (66000)^{-3}</mrow>
                        <mrow>\amp = 1 - \left( \frac{44000}{66000} \right)^3</mrow>
                        <mrow>\amp = 1 - \left( \frac{2}{3} \right)^3</mrow>
                        <mrow>\amp = 1 - \frac{8}{27}</mrow>
                        <mrow>\amp = \frac{19}{27} \approx 0.7037</mrow>
                    </md>
                    Thus, approximately <m>70.37\%</m> (or roughly 70%) of the cables possess a breaking strength below the statistical mean.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
    </exercise>




    
    


    <exercise label="ex-butterfly-lifespan-analysis">
        <title>Ecological Lifespan Analysis</title>
<introduction>
    <p>
        The lifespan of a butterfly after emerging from its chrysalis can be modeled by a continuous random variable <m>T</m> (measured in days). The model defines the probability that a butterfly survives for longer than <m>t</m> days using the survival function:
        <me>P(T \gt t) = \frac{36}{(6+t)^2}, \quad \text{for } t \ge 0</me>
        
    </p>
    </introduction>
        <!-- Task A: Probability of dying within 6 days -->
        <task xml:id="task-butterfly-early-mortality">
            <statement>
                <p>
                     What is the probability that a butterfly will die within 6 days of emerging from its chrysalis? 
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    The event that a butterfly dies within 6 days corresponds to the cumulative probability <m>P(T \le 6)</m>. Using the complement of the given survival function:
                    <md>
                        <mrow>P(T \le 6) \amp = 1 - P(T \gt 6)</mrow>
                        <mrow>\amp = 1 - \frac{36}{(6+6)^2}</mrow>
                        <mrow>\amp = 1 - \frac{36}{12^2}</mrow>
                        <mrow>\amp = 1 - \frac{36}{144} = 1 - 0.25 = 0.75</mrow>
                    </md>
                    Thus, the model predicts that exactly 75% of the emerged population will die within the first 6 days.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <!-- Task B: Survival Percentile Threshold -->
        <task xml:id="task-butterfly-survival-threshold">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    If a large number of butterflies emerges on the same day, after how many days would you expect only <m>6\%</m> to remain alive?
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    We are searching for the specific time target <m>t</m> where the survival probability drops to <m>0.06</m>, meaning <m>P(T \gt t) = 0.06</m>. Reorganizing the algebraic model:
                    <md>
                        <mrow>\frac{36}{(6+t)^2} \amp = 0.06</mrow>
                        <mrow>(6+t)^2 \amp = \frac{36}{0.06}</mrow>
                        <mrow>(6+t)^2 \amp = 600</mrow>
                    </md>
                    Taking the positive square root of both sides to isolate our time variable:
                    <md>
                        <mrow>6 + t \amp = \sqrt{600} \approx 24.495</mrow>
                        <mrow>t \amp = 24.495 - 6 \approx 18.5 \text{ days}</mrow>
                    </md>
                    Therefore, it takes approximately 18.5 days for the surviving population cohort to dwindle down to 6%.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <!-- Task C: Mean Lifetime Derivation -->
        <task xml:id="task-butterfly-mean-lifetime">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find the mean (expected) lifetime of a butterfly after emerging from its chrysalis.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    Because <m>T</m> is a non-negative continuous random variable (<m>T \ge 0</m>), we can find its expected value by directly integrating its survival function over the interval <m>[0, \infty)</m>:
                    <me>E(T) = \int_{0}^{\infty} P(T \gt t) \, dt = \int_{0}^{\infty} \frac{36}{(6+t)^2} \, dt</me>
                    Evaluating the improper integral using anti-derivatives:
                    <md>
                        <mrow>E(T) \amp = \lim_{b \to \infty} \int_{0}^{b} 36(6+t)^{-2} \, dt</mrow>
                        <mrow>\amp = \lim_{b \to \infty} \left[ -\frac{36}{6+t} \right]_{0}^{b}</mrow>
                        <mrow>\amp = \lim_{b \to \infty} \left( -\frac{36}{6+b} - \left( -\frac{36}{6+0} \right) \right)</mrow>
                        <mrow>\amp = 0 - (-6) = 6 \text{ days}</mrow>
                    </md>
                    The expected mean lifetime for an emerged butterfly is exactly 6 days.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
    </exercise>


